  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n- Heavy Rain Potential Continues Tonight into Sunday morning\n\nWe will be maintaining the Flood Watch as is with this forecast\nupdate. As expected, the heaviest rain that has already fallen \nhas occurred across the 5 counties that are in the watch. Ground \ntruth reports show that the heaviest rain has been across Allegan \nCounty with a number of reports in the 2-3 inch range. Additional\nrain is expected tonight, so the Watch continues. Given the\nsaturated ground additional rainfall will go directly into \nrunoff. \n\nThe setup largely remains the same with a diffuse surface boundary\nto our south and waves of low pressure working eastward towards\nthe Great Lakes. PWAT values are up around 2.00 inches which are\nhigh even for mid summer. High resolution models show the low\nlevel jet aimed at our far southern CWA towards I-94. This should\nbe the focus area for heavy rain tonight based on the moisture\ntransport vectors centered in that area as well. So, the Flood\nWatch area stands the best chance to see flooding/heavy rain. \n\nthe first wave of precipitation will work in form the west after\n500pm with more substantial rain after dark and especially\novernight. The rain moves southward on Sunday as high pressure\nbegins to slide in from the north. \n\n- Largely Dry for the Upcoming Work Week\n\nWe will largely be dry this upcoming week as high pressure will be\nsituated across the Great Lakes region. Aloft however there is an\nupper low meandering through the region, so it not inconceivable\nthat we see some scattered showers that will be diurnally driven\nin the afternoon and evening. Deep moisture is not present though\ngiven the surface high.\n\n- Near to Slightly Below Normal Temperatures Next 7 Day\n\nIt will feel like fall this week with most days seeing high\ntemperatures in the 60s. Normal highs for today in Grand Rapids\ntoday are in the lower 70s which cool to the upper 60s by next \nweekend.",
